<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 3.1, Essential 3.1 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-statistics-ii" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title component="core">Statistics II</title>
  <title component="essential">Statistics III</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      You have already collected data, organised it in frequency tables, drawn graphs and found the mean, median and mode. An average alone does not tell the whole story: two classes can have the same mean mark while one has marks that are far more spread out than the other.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will calculate the mean, variance and standard deviation of data, first ungrouped and then grouped. You will draw cumulative frequency curves, find the median and quartiles by calculation and from the curve, and use measures of dispersion such as the interquartile range and the standard deviation to describe how spread out the data is.
    </p>
    <p>
      These measures are used to compare examination results, rainfall in different counties, crop yields and prices, so that decisions rest on evidence.
    </p>
  </introduction>

